Usage: codeskeptic [options] [source_path]

Options:
  --source <path>        Directory/file to analyze
  --build-path <path>    compile_commands.json directory
  --json <file>          JSON output file
  --sarif <file>         SARIF 2.1.0 output file
  --html <file>          Self-contained HTML report (filters,
                         dataflow traces with source context)
  --severity <level>     Minimum severity (info/warning/error)
  --disable-rule <id>    Disable a rule
  --baseline <file>      Suppress findings recorded in baseline
  --write-baseline <file> Record current findings as baseline
  --function <names>     Analyze only these functions (comma list,
                         plain or qualified; repeatable)
  --lines <N-M,K>        Analyze only functions overlapping these
                         line ranges of the analyzed file
  --fatal-asserts <names> Treat these functions as never returning
                         (comma list; kills dataflow paths after
                         custom assert-failure handlers that lack
                         [[noreturn]])
  --assert-macros <names> Extra macro names that are assertions
                         (comma list). Anything containing
                         "assert" is recognized already; use this
                         for CHECK/VERIFY-style spellings. Single-
                         argument macros only. Never list one that
                         asserts a NEGATIVE (assert_null and
                         friends) - it would be believed backwards
  --negative-assert-macros <names> Macro names that assert a
                         pointer IS null/empty/unset (comma list).
                         The spelling heuristic vetoes a null-ness
                         vocabulary already; list here any negative
                         macro whose name uses none of those words
                         (wins over --assert-macros on conflict)
  --no-assert-recovery   Do not recover assert conditions that
                         NDEBUG compiled out. On by default: a
                         release-build assert() leaves no trace in
                         the AST, so without recovery every
                         pointer it guards reads as unchecked
  --alloc-functions <names> Treat these functions as heap
                         allocators (comma list; extends the
                         leak/double-free/UAF analysis to project
                         wrappers like git__malloc, zmalloc)
  --untrusted-int-sources <names> Treat these functions'
                         return AND integer out-params as a
                         full-range untrusted length (comma list;
                         same discipline as atoi — for wire/packet
                         length fields in parsers; also drives the
                         sign-conversion rule)
  --free-functions <names> Treat these functions as deallocators
                         (first argument is freed)
  --allocator-pairs <pairs> Exact custom allocation families
                         (allocator=deallocator, comma list;
                         malformed values are rejected)
  --owning-pointers <names> Treat these class templates as
                         owning smart pointers (comma list;
                         a raw pointer adopted by constructing
                         one — Ref, RefPtr, scoped_refptr — is
                         no longer leaked; std::unique_ptr/
                         shared_ptr are built in)
  --report-paths <paths> Report only findings under these
                         path prefixes (comma list). Filters
                         out findings in dependency headers
                         pulled into your TUs; analysis is
                         unaffected
  --serve                Run as an MCP server (JSON-RPC on stdio)
  --whole-program        Two-pass mode: collect function summaries
                         across all files first, then analyze
  --accept-partial-coverage  Legacy acknowledgement flag; partial
                         TU coverage still has no verdict (exit 2)
  --tu-timeout-seconds <N> Per-translation-unit wall timeout
                         (default: 300; range: 1-86400)
  --tu-memory-mib <N>   Per-translation-unit memory ceiling in MiB
                         (default: 4096; range: 1-131072)
  --summary-out <file>   Save harvested cross-file function
                         summaries to a file after analysis
  --summary-in <file>    Load function summaries saved earlier;
                         analyze single files with whole-project
                         knowledge (incremental whole-program)
  --model-file <file>    Load an opt-in library model using the
                         strict summary schema (repeatable;
                         malformed or missing files exit 2)
  --summary-diff <old> <new>  Report contract changes between two
